<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Horea</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1731">1731</a>–<a href="/years/1785">1785</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Vasile Ursu Nicola</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Horea, born Vasile Ursu Nicola, was a Romanian serf who led the peasant revolt of 1784 in the Apuseni Mountains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a> together with Cloșca and Crișan. The rebels rose against labour dues and Hungarian landlords, attacking manors across the region. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> troops suppressed the revolt, and Horea and Cloșca were broken on the wheel at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alba-iulia">Alba Iulia</a> in February 1785.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/horea.jpg" alt="Horea" width="280" height="357" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chc_horea.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Horea was born to poor peasants in the village of Arada in Țara Moților, which now bears his name. His baptismal name, Ursu (bear), followed an old custom of naming children after strong animals, and his nickname came from a flute-like instrument, the horea, that he played as a youth. He married Ilina and had two sons, Ion and Luca. A builder of wooden churches, he left one at Cizer, dated 1773 and now in the National Museum of Transylvanian History in Cluj-Napoca, and he was probably literate although a serf.</p><p>Since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a> passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> in 1691, its Orthodox Romanian peasants had no political representation, while Hungarians, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szekelys">Székelys</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> enjoyed rights and privileges. They were serfs without personal freedom or support for their church, and they paid heavy taxes to mostly Hungarian Catholic nobles and to the emperor.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Horea became the peasants' spokesman and travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> four times between 1779 and 1784 to put their grievances to Emperor Joseph II in person, at times with Cloșca. That a serf was received suggests supporters at court; one source names the geologist Ignatius Born, on whose estates Horea worked. Joseph was then in conflict with the Transylvanian nobility, and Horea is said to have told the serfs that the emperor had authorised them to wipe out the Hungarian nobles.</p><p>The rising began on 31 October 1784, when about 600 peasants met with Crișan and Cloșca at Mesteacăn and were shown a golden cross said to have come from the emperor. The rebels destroyed many castles and manor houses and massacred nobles and Catholics, most of them Hungarians, while sparing Austrian soldiers and imperial property; captured nobles were freed if they became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a>. On 11 November Horea demanded that the nobility be abolished and its estates divided among the people. After a truce failed, the Austrians defeated the peasants at Mihăileni on 7 December, and Horea and Cloșca, betrayed in the Gilău Mountains, were arrested on 27 December.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Joseph II pardoned the other condemned rebels and ordered only the three leaders executed. Crișan hanged himself in prison, and after Horea and Cloșca died on the wheel their bodies were cut up and shown in villages as a warning. In 1785 the emperor ordered an end to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a>. Horea became a Romanian folk hero, the subject of Nicolae Bretan's 1937 opera Horia and a 1984 film.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vasile Ursu Nicola” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Horea's</b> peasant revolt of 1784 was bloodily suppressed.</blockquote><a href="/state/grand-principality-of-transylvania">Grand Principality of Transylvania</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Horea, in what is now <a href="/countries/romania">Romania</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alba-iulia">Alba Iulia</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/romania">Romania</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=46.52,22.95&amp;year=1766" aria-label="Horea on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Horea"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4586 6120h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4586 6120h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=46.52,22.95&amp;year=1766">What was here in 1766? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
